TITLE:: PltWave
summary:: Waveform of recorded material, as min and max per pixel column
categories:: Libraries>PlotLib
related:: Overviews/PlotLib, Classes/PltSpectrogram, Classes/PltVector, Classes/Signal

DESCRIPTION::
One vertical line per pixel column, from the lowest to the highest sample in that
column, with the RMS drawn inside it. Multichannel material gets one panel per
channel, stacked, sharing the time axis.

link::Classes/Signal#-plot:: draws every sample, which is right for 512 samples and
unusable for five million. A min and max per column stays legible at any length and
never drops a peak, which a line through every Nth sample does.

CLASSMETHODS::

METHOD:: new
argument:: data
A collection of numbers for one channel, or a collection of those for several:
code::PltWave([left, right], 48000)::.
argument:: sampleRate
Only used to label the time axis.
argument:: title
argument:: columns
argument:: width
argument:: height

METHOD:: fromSoundFile
Read a file in chunks, so a long one never has to be in memory at once. The window
is titled with the file name.
argument:: path
argument:: columns
argument:: title
argument:: width
argument:: height

METHOD:: fromBuffer
A buffer on the server, which arrives asynchronously, so this one answers through
code::action::.
code::
PltWave.fromBuffer(b, action: { |plot| plot.front });
::

METHOD:: envelope
The envelope of one channel: code::(mins:, maxs:, rms:)::, one entry per column.
Columns are never more numerous than samples, so short material gives fewer columns
rather than empty ones.

METHOD:: envelopeFromSoundFile
The same, read from a file in chunks.
argument:: path
argument:: columns
argument:: startTime
argument:: endTime
argument:: samplesPerColumn
Bounds the work. sclang would spend a long time visiting every sample of a long
file, so past this limit it visits an evenly spread subset instead. The stride used
comes back in the result and is printed in the caption, never applied silently.
returns:: code::(channels:, duration:, sampleRate:, numChannels:, stride:, startTime:)::

INSTANCEMETHODS::

METHOD:: zoom
Recompute over a time range. Needs a soundfile source: data handed in directly has
already been reduced to columns and cannot be looked into more closely.
code::
~path = Platform.resourceDir +/+ "sounds/a11wlk01.wav";
w = PltWave.fromSoundFile(~path).front;
w.zoom(1.0, 1.4);
w.zoom(0);
::

METHOD:: dbScale
An amplitude axis, or a dB one where the column peak is filled and the RMS drawn
over it. The distance between the two is the crest factor.

METHOD:: peakDb
Peak of the whole plot, in dB.

METHOD:: showRms
METHOD:: floorDb
Bottom of the dB scale.
METHOD:: panelGap
Pixels between stacked channel panels.
METHOD:: traceColorKey
METHOD:: rmsColorKey
Theme keys rather than colours, so a theme change moves them.
METHOD:: channels
The envelopes, one Event per channel.
METHOD:: duration
METHOD:: sampleRate
METHOD:: numChannels
METHOD:: stride

EXAMPLES::
code::
// a file
PltWave.fromSoundFile(Platform.resourceDir +/+ "sounds/a11wlk01.wav").front;

// in dB, where the quiet parts of a recording are legible
(
PltWave.fromSoundFile(Platform.resourceDir +/+ "sounds/a11wlk01.wav")
	.dbScale_(true).front;
)

// a synthesised stereo pair
(
PltWave([
	Signal.fill(88200, { |i| sin(i * 0.02) * (1 - (i / 88200)) }),
	Signal.fill(88200, { |i| sin(i * 0.031) * ((i / 88200) ** 2) })
], 44100, "decay and swell").front;
)

// data only, no window
PltWave.envelope(Signal.sineFill(4096, [1, 0, 0.3]), 200);
::
